  "query_preview": "..\nWednesday to Thursday: A changing weather pattern will be coming \nto the Inland NW the next couple days, with isolated \nshower/t-storm chances, some winds and elevated to critical fire \nweather conditions. Today will remain hot, with highs in the 90s \nand locally lower 100s before a cool down starts Thursday. A broad\ntrough sits off the coast and the southerly flow will carry a \ndeformation axis northward. That feature will couple with more\ninstability to bring isolated shower and thunderstorms. Some \nmodels start to develop the risk near 9-11 AM near the \nSpokane/C'dA area before lifting north, but the overall risk is \nonly near 10% in Spokane. Chances increase from 11 am to 8 pm \nover the northeast mountains, but in general the models are only\nshow speckled isolated convection. It could be underdone. The \nStorm Prediction Center was painting all north WA and the ID \nPanhandle, clipping the eastern third of WA with the risk. This \noverall is not expected to be much impact, but it will need \nmonitoring. Wind will be on the increase, especially near the \nCascades and central WA. Some breezy condition expected east \ntoo, but the winds and dry RH value over central WA will lead to\nelevated fire weather conditions. As we head into Thursday a \ndry cold front and upper trough moves by, increasing winds \nfurther bringing elevated to critical conditions throughout the \narea. The most critical risk is over the Okanogan Valley south \nin the afternoon, with winds 15-20 mph and gusts to 35 mph \npossible, with dry RH. Active wildfires remain in that area and \nsome increase activity will be possible in this set-up. A fire \nweather watch is in place for Thursday.\n\nFriday to Tuesday: A transition to a cooler pattern comes \nFriday and continues into the weekend, with highs below normal \nin many areas. Some shower risk pushes up to the Cascade crest \non Friday, but overall it will be dry. Breezy conditions \ncontinue periodically through this period. A passing disturbance\nin the broader trough will bring some shower/t-storm chances \nnorth Saturday. Friday and Saturday remain breezy, but elevated \nfire weather conditions are less prominent at this time. Winds \nsubside some Sunday, then additional waves round in Monday and \nTuesday. Some shower chance push to the Cascade crest Monday and\ninto the northern mountains Tuesday. Winds will increase \nslightly and RH value start to decrease so we will have to \nmonitor Monday and Tuesday for elevated fire weather conditions\nover central WA with the dry and breezy conditions. \nTemperatures cool to the 80s Friday and 70 this weekend, with \nmoderating slightly thereafter but still near to slightly below \nnormal. /Solveig",
